  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 255 PM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nA surface ridge of high pressure is shifting off the to the east \nthis afternoon and modest WAA has developed. That has allow for \ntemperatures to warm into the low to mid 80s this afternoon. \nTonight, a upper level trough will move from the northern Plains \ninto the northern Great Lakes. This will force a trailing cold front \ninto the area tomorrow morning. A few showers and storms may be \npossible along and ahead of this front across the western CWA \ntomorrow morning (20-30%). Tomorrow, the overall pattern will \nfeature quasi-zonal flow aloft with a stalled frontal boundary \nbisecting the area. During the late afternoon/evening hours a upper \nlevel shortwave traveling on zonal flow aloft is progged to move \ninto the area which is expected to kick off another round of showers \nand thunderstorms with the stalled frontal boundary as the focus. \nThese storms are expected to develop in an environment where 2500-\n3500J/Kg of MUCAPE will be available and 40-45kts of 0-6Km shear. \nThis will provide the potential for storms to be strong to severe \nwith large hail and damaging winds the main threats. Tomorrow night, \na southwestern LLJ will develop and nose into the area which will \ncontinue the chance for stronger storms as well as the potential for \nheavy rain with PWATs hovering in the 1.75-2.00\" range. As such, \nlocalized flooding cannot be ruled out. Storm chances will continue \ninto Sunday as quasi-zonal flow persists with several subtle \nshortwave possible. This will continue the potential for flooding \nparticularly across the northern CWA where storms are more likely. \nHighs Saturday will be in the 80s. The temperatures on Sunday will \nhave a greater spread with cloud cover and storms keeping highs in \nthe mid 70s over northern Missouri but reaching the upper 80s across \nthe southern CWA.\n\nSunday night into Monday, upper level ridging will build over the \nMississippi River Valley. As a warm front lifts north of the area. \nThis will aid in drying conditions out and allow highs to rise into \nthe mid 80s to mid 90s. Tuesday the upper level ridge axis move east \ninto the Tennessee and Ohio River Valleys. This will leave the \nlocal area on the periphery of the ridge. Several shortwaves \nrounding the ridge into the local area in the Tuesday through \nThursday timeframe looks to set up a very wet and unsettled middle \npart of the next work week.",
